PRIVATE REFLECTION: {I continue to favor option C as the best answer, with the caveat that its final phrase (“compared to alcohol-preferring inhibition”) is almost certainly a typo for “compared to sucrose-preferring rats.” The central mechanism is that Slc6a11 encodes the GABA transporter GAT-3. Knockdown of Slc6a11 in sucrose-preferring rats mimics the alcohol-preferring amygdala electrophysiological phenotype (shRNA −0.37 mV vs. scrambled −0.16 mV; alcohol-preferring −0.38 mV vs. sucrose-preferring −0.17 mV). This strongly implies reduced GABA reuptake in alcohol-preferring rats, leading to higher extracellular GABA, more active GABA receptors, and increased tonic GABAergic inhibition in the amygdala. It also supports the therapeutic inference that a GABA receptor agonist, such as a baclofen-like agent, may decrease alcohol self-administration.

In the current round, nearly all colleagues converged on C. Colleague 10, who initially voted B, switched to C after recognizing that GABA receptor antagonists are inconsistent with enhanced GABAergic tone. Colleague 16 still leans toward D as written because C contains the nonsensical typo, but agrees C would be equivalent if corrected. D is close but less complete because it omits the explicit “higher extracellular GABA” point. B is weaker because it proposes GABA receptor antagonists may reduce alcohol self-administration, opposite to the expected direction if alcohol-preferring rats already have increased tonic inhibition. Other options incorrectly assign increased tonic inhibition to sucrose-preferring rats or claim decreased GABA/neuronal activity in the wrong direction.

Unresolved caveat: the sign/meaning of population spike amplitude could be debated, since more negative amplitude might conventionally suggest altered excitability rather than simply increased inhibition. However, the Slc6a11/GAT-3 knockdown logic and the answer-choice structure strongly point to increased GABAergic inhibition and GABA agonist utility as the intended conclusion. Therefore my current vote remains C, with D as the closest alternative only if C’s typo is taken literally.}

